from sc_lib import *
# ---- 1 Security and compliance concepts
C(1,"Shared responsibility: what is ALWAYS the customer's job?","Your **information and data**, your **devices** (PCs and mobile), and your **accounts and identities**. This holds for IaaS, PaaS and SaaS.")
C(1,"Shared responsibility: what is ALWAYS Microsoft's job in the cloud?","The **physical datacenter**, the **physical network** and the **physical hosts**.")
C(1,"Name the seven defense-in-depth layers, outside to inside.","**Physical, Identity and access, Perimeter, Network, Compute, Application, Data.** Each layer slows an attacker if the one before it fails.")
C(1,"What is the CIA triad?","**Confidentiality** (only the right people see it), **Integrity** (it is not altered), **Availability** (it is there when needed).")
C(1,"Zero Trust: the three guiding principles?","**Verify explicitly** (always authenticate and authorize on all data points), **use least privilege access** (just-in-time, just-enough), **assume breach** (segment, encrypt, monitor).")
C(1,"Zero Trust: the six foundational pillars?","**Identities, Devices (endpoints), Applications, Data, Infrastructure, Networks.**")
C(1,"Symmetric vs asymmetric encryption?","**Symmetric:** one key encrypts and decrypts. **Asymmetric:** a public and private key pair. What one key encrypts, only the other decrypts.")
C(1,"Encryption at rest, in transit, in use?","**At rest:** stored data (disk, database). **In transit:** moving over a network (HTTPS/TLS). **In use:** data being processed in memory.")
C(1,"What is hashing, and how is it different from encryption?","Hashing turns data into a **fixed-length value** and is **one-way**: you cannot reverse it. The same input always gives the same hash. Used to store passwords and check integrity. **Salting** adds a random value so identical passwords get different hashes.")
C(1,"GRC: what do the three letters cover?","**Governance:** the rules and processes that direct the organization. **Risk management:** identify, assess and respond to threats. **Compliance:** follow the laws and regulations that apply.")
C(1,"Data residency vs data sovereignty?","**Residency:** rules about the physical place data may be stored. **Sovereignty:** data is subject to the laws of the country or region where it is collected, held or processed.")
Q(1,"A company moves its email to a SaaS service. Which is still the company's responsibility?","The accounts and identities used to sign in",["Patching the mail server operating system","Physical security of the datacenter","Maintaining the physical network"],"Customers always keep responsibility for data, devices, and accounts and identities, even in SaaS.")
Q(1,"A file's contents were changed by an attacker without anyone noticing. Which part of the CIA triad was broken?","Integrity",["Confidentiality","Availability","Accountability"],"Integrity means data is not altered without authorization. Confidentiality is about who can see it, availability about access when needed.")
Q(1,"Which Zero Trust principle is met by limiting admins to just-in-time and just-enough access?","Use least privilege access",["Verify explicitly","Assume breach","Defense in depth"],"Least privilege limits access with JIT and JEA. Verify explicitly is about authenticating on all signals. Assume breach is about segmenting and monitoring.")
Q(1,"You need to store user passwords so that the original password cannot be recovered from the stored value. What should you use?","Hashing with a salt",["Symmetric encryption","Asymmetric encryption","Encryption in transit"],"Hashing is one-way. Encryption can be reversed with the key, so it does not meet the requirement.")
Q(1,"Which defense-in-depth layer do DDoS protection and firewalls at the edge of the network belong to?","Perimeter",["Compute","Application","Data"],"The perimeter layer guards against network-based attacks from outside, such as DDoS. The network layer is about segmentation inside.")
Q(1,"Which TWO are Zero Trust guiding principles?",["Verify explicitly","Assume breach"],["Trust the corporate network","Encrypt only data at rest"],"The three principles are verify explicitly, use least privilege access and assume breach. Zero Trust never trusts a network location by itself.")
YN(1,["In asymmetric encryption, data encrypted with the public key can be decrypted with the same public key.","A hash value can be decrypted to get the original data back.","HTTPS is an example of encryption in transit."],[0,0,1],"Asymmetric encryption needs the other key of the pair. Hashing is one-way. HTTPS (TLS) protects data moving across a network.")
M(1,"Match each example to the defense-in-depth layer.",[("Badge access to the datacenter building","Physical"),("Multifactor authentication for sign-in","Identity and access"),("Patching virtual machine operating systems","Compute"),("Encrypting a database","Data")],"Physical is the building. Identity and access controls who signs in. Compute covers VMs and endpoints. Data is the innermost layer.")
D(1,"Encryption","Hashing","One-way. Cannot be reversed to get the original.",1,"Hashing is one-way and fixed-length. Encryption can be reversed with the right key.")
D(1,"Symmetric encryption","Asymmetric encryption","Uses a public and private key pair.",1,"Asymmetric uses a key pair. Symmetric uses one shared key.")
D(1,"Data residency","Data sovereignty","Data falls under the laws of the country where it is held.",1,"Sovereignty is about which laws apply. Residency is about where data may physically be stored.")
D(1,"Verify explicitly","Assume breach","Segment access and monitor so an intruder cannot spread.",1,"Assume breach minimizes blast radius. Verify explicitly checks every request on all available signals.")
# ---- 2 Identity concepts
C(2,"Why is identity called the primary security perimeter?","Cloud apps, personal devices and remote work mean the corporate network is no longer the edge. **Who** is signing in (person, service or device) is what you can check everywhere.")
C(2,"The four pillars of an identity infrastructure?","**Administration** (create and manage identities), **Authentication** (prove who you are), **Authorization** (what you may do), **Auditing** (track who did what).")
C(2,"Authentication vs authorization?","**Authentication (AuthN):** proving you are who you say you are. **Authorization (AuthZ):** deciding what an authenticated identity is allowed to access.")
C(2,"What is an identity provider (IdP)?","A service that **creates, stores and manages identities** and handles authentication, authorization and auditing for apps. With modern authentication the IdP issues a **security token** that apps trust. Microsoft Entra ID is one.")
C(2,"What is single sign-on (SSO)?","Sign in **once** and use that one credential to reach many apps and resources, without signing in again for each.")
C(2,"What is a directory service? Name Microsoft's on-premises one.","A hierarchical store of objects on a network (users, devices, groups). **Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)** is the on-premises one. It does not natively support mobile devices, SaaS apps or modern authentication.")
C(2,"What is federation?","A **trust relationship between identity providers** in different organizations or domains, so users sign in with their home identity. Trust is **not always two-way**.")
Q(2,"A user enters a password and then approves a prompt on their phone. Which process is this?","Authentication",["Authorization","Auditing","Federation"],"Proving who you are is authentication. Authorization happens afterwards and decides what you can access.")
Q(2,"After sign-in, a user can read a SharePoint site but cannot edit it. Which process decided that?","Authorization",["Authentication","Administration","Single sign-on"],"Authorization sets the level of access an authenticated identity gets.")
Q(2,"Users at Contoso sign in to a partner's app with their Contoso accounts. The partner's identity provider trusts Contoso's. What is this called?","Federation",["Multifactor authentication","Password hash synchronization","Conditional Access"],"Federation is a trust relationship between identity providers across organizations.")
Q(2,"Which statement about Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) is true?","It is an on-premises directory service that does not natively support SaaS apps and modern authentication",["It is the cloud identity provider included with Microsoft 365","It only stores device objects","It replaces the need for any authentication"],"AD DS is the on-premises directory. Microsoft Entra ID is the cloud identity provider built for modern authentication.")
Q(2,"With modern authentication, what does the identity provider give the client after a successful sign-in?","A security token",["The user's password","A firewall rule","An encryption key for the disk"],"The identity provider issues a security token that the client presents to apps. Apps trust the token, not the password.")
Q(2,"Which TWO are pillars of an identity infrastructure?",["Authentication","Auditing"],["Virtualization","Encryption at rest"],"The four pillars are administration, authentication, authorization and auditing.")
YN(2,["Authorization happens before authentication.","In a federation, trust between two identity providers is always bidirectional.","Single sign-on lets a user sign in once and access multiple applications."],[0,0,1],"Authentication comes first. Federation trust can be one-way. SSO means one sign-in for many apps.")
D(2,"Authentication","Authorization","Decides what you are allowed to do.",1,"Authorization grants access levels. Authentication proves identity.")
D(2,"Active Directory Domain Services","Microsoft Entra ID","Cloud identity provider built for modern authentication.",1,"Entra ID is cloud-based. AD DS is the on-premises directory.")
D(2,"Single sign-on","Federation","A trust between identity providers of different organizations.",1,"Federation is the trust. SSO is the one-sign-in experience it can enable.")
# ---- 3 Entra ID and identity types
C(3,"What is Microsoft Entra ID?","Microsoft's **cloud-based identity and access management** service. Microsoft 365, Azure and Dynamics 365 tenants use it automatically. Formerly Azure Active Directory.")
C(3,"The main identity types in Microsoft Entra ID?","**Human** (workforce employees, and external guests, partners, customers), **workload** (applications, service principals, managed identities), **device**, and newer **agent identities** for AI agents.")
C(3,"What is a service principal?","The identity an **application** uses in a tenant, so it can be authenticated and given permissions. You must manage its credentials unless it is a managed identity.")
C(3,"What is a managed identity? Two kinds?","A service principal that **Azure manages for you**: no credentials to store or rotate. **System-assigned:** tied to one resource and deleted with it. **User-assigned:** standalone, can be shared by several resources.")
C(3,"Device identity: registered vs joined vs hybrid joined?","**Entra registered:** personal (BYOD) device, sign in with a personal or local account. **Entra joined:** organization-owned, sign in with a work account, cloud only. **Hybrid joined:** organization-owned, joined to on-premises AD and registered in Entra.")
C(3,"What does Microsoft Entra External ID B2B collaboration do?","Lets **guests** (partners, vendors) sign in with **their own identity** to use your apps. You manage their access, they manage their credentials.")
C(3,"What is hybrid identity, and what makes it work?","One identity for on-premises and cloud. **Microsoft Entra Connect Sync** or **Cloud Sync** synchronizes on-premises Active Directory with Microsoft Entra ID.")
C(3,"Hybrid sign-in: password hash sync vs pass-through authentication vs federation?","**Password hash sync:** a hash of the hash is stored in Entra, sign-in happens in the cloud, simplest. **Pass-through:** an on-premises agent validates the password, nothing stored in the cloud. **Federation:** Entra hands off to a separate trusted identity provider such as AD FS.")
Q(3,"An Azure VM needs to read secrets from Key Vault. You do not want to store or rotate any credentials. What should you use?","A managed identity",["A guest user account","A shared administrator password","A device identity"],"Managed identities are managed by Azure, so there are no credentials in code or to rotate.")
Q(3,"A managed identity must be used by five different virtual machines and should exist independently of them. Which type fits?","User-assigned managed identity",["System-assigned managed identity","Guest identity","Hybrid joined device"],"User-assigned identities are standalone and can be shared. System-assigned is tied to exactly one resource.")
Q(3,"An employee wants to use a personal phone for work email. Which device identity type fits a personal device?","Microsoft Entra registered",["Microsoft Entra joined","Microsoft Entra hybrid joined","Managed identity"],"Registered is for personally owned (BYOD) devices. Joined and hybrid joined are for organization-owned devices.")
Q(3,"A company wants on-premises AD users to sign in to Microsoft 365 with the same password, and wants sign-in to keep working if the on-premises servers go down. Which method fits?","Password hash synchronization",["Pass-through authentication","Federation with AD FS","Security defaults"],"With password hash sync, authentication happens in the cloud, so it does not depend on on-premises servers. Pass-through and federation both need on-premises components at sign-in.")
Q(3,"A vendor's staff must access your SharePoint site using their own company accounts. What should you use?","Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration",["Microsoft Entra joined devices","A system-assigned managed identity","Pass-through authentication"],"B2B collaboration invites external users as guests who sign in with their own identity.")
Q(3,"Which TWO are workload identities?",["Service principal","Managed identity"],["Guest user","Microsoft Entra registered device"],"Workload identities are for software: applications, service principals and managed identities. Guests are human identities.")
M(3,"Match each description to the identity type.",[("An employee's work account","Workforce user identity"),("A partner invited as a guest","External identity"),("An app that authenticates to call an API","Workload identity"),("A company laptop joined to Entra","Device identity")],"Human identities are workforce or external. Workload identities are for software. Devices have their own identity.")
D(3,"System-assigned managed identity","User-assigned managed identity","Deleted automatically when its one resource is deleted.",0,"System-assigned shares the lifecycle of one resource. User-assigned is standalone.")
D(3,"Microsoft Entra registered","Microsoft Entra joined","Organization-owned device signed in with a work account.",1,"Joined is for organization-owned devices. Registered is for personal devices.")
D(3,"Password hash synchronization","Pass-through authentication","Password is validated by an on-premises agent. Nothing stored in the cloud.",1,"Pass-through validates on-premises. Password hash sync stores a hash of the hash in Entra.")
# ---- 4 Authentication
C(4,"What is multifactor authentication (MFA)? The three factor types?","Sign-in that needs **two or more** of: something you **know** (password, PIN), something you **have** (phone, security key), something you **are** (fingerprint, face).")
C(4,"Which authentication methods are passwordless in Microsoft Entra ID?","**Windows Hello for Business**, **Microsoft Authenticator** (phone sign-in), and **passkeys (FIDO2)** security keys.")
C(4,"Why is a Windows Hello PIN safer than a password?","The PIN is **tied to that one device** and backed by its TPM chip. It is never sent to a server, so a stolen PIN is useless without the device.")
C(4,"What are security defaults?","Free, preconfigured baseline settings: **all users register for MFA**, **administrators must use MFA**, and **legacy authentication is blocked**. For organizations not using Conditional Access.")
C(4,"What is self-service password reset (SSPR)?","Users **change, reset or unlock** their own password with no help desk call, after proving identity with registered methods. Cuts help desk cost and downtime.")
C(4,"Password protection: global vs custom banned password list?","**Global:** maintained by Microsoft from real attack data, applied to everyone automatically. **Custom:** your own terms (brand, product names, locations). Both can also protect on-premises AD in a hybrid setup.")
C(4,"Rank these by strength: password only, password + SMS, passwordless.","Weakest: **password only**. Better: **password + second factor**. Best: **passwordless** (Windows Hello, Authenticator, passkey).")
C(4,"Which methods can be used as a second factor in Entra MFA?","Microsoft Authenticator, passkeys (FIDO2), Windows Hello for Business, OATH hardware or software tokens, SMS and voice call. A **password plus a security question is NOT MFA** in Entra sign-in.")
Q(4,"A user signs in with a password and a PIN they memorized. Is this multifactor authentication?","No, both are something you know",["Yes, because two items were entered","Yes, a PIN is something you have","No, MFA requires three factors"],"MFA needs factors from different categories. A password and a memorized PIN are both knowledge factors.")
Q(4,"Which sign-in method is passwordless?","A FIDO2 passkey on a security key",["A password plus an SMS code","A password plus a voice call","A complex 20-character password"],"Passkeys (FIDO2), Windows Hello for Business and Microsoft Authenticator phone sign-in remove the password.")
Q(4,"A small company has no Conditional Access licenses but wants MFA for all users and legacy authentication blocked. What should it turn on?","Security defaults",["Privileged Identity Management","Microsoft Entra ID Protection","Password hash synchronization"],"Security defaults give a free baseline: MFA registration for all, MFA for admins, legacy authentication blocked.")
Q(4,"Help desk calls for forgotten passwords are costing too much. Which feature lets users fix this themselves?","Self-service password reset",["Custom banned password list","Access reviews","Microsoft Entra Connect"],"SSPR lets users reset or unlock their own accounts after verifying identity.")
Q(4,"You want to stop users choosing passwords that contain your company name. What should you configure?","Custom banned password list",["Global banned password list","Security defaults","Smart lockout"],"The custom list holds organization-specific terms. The global list is maintained by Microsoft and cannot be edited.")
Q(4,"Which TWO are authentication factors that count as something you have?",["A registered phone running Microsoft Authenticator","A FIDO2 security key"],["A fingerprint","A password"],"A phone or key is something you have. A fingerprint is something you are. A password is something you know.")
YN(4,["Windows Hello for Business replaces passwords with a credential tied to the device.","Self-service password reset requires a help desk agent to approve each reset.","The global banned password list is applied automatically to all users."],[1,0,1],"Windows Hello uses a device-bound credential unlocked by PIN or biometrics. SSPR needs no help desk. The global list is automatic.")
D(4,"Global banned password list","Custom banned password list","You add your own company and product names.",1,"Custom holds your terms. Global is maintained by Microsoft.")
D(4,"Security defaults","Conditional Access","Free baseline that turns on MFA for everyone with no policy design.",0,"Security defaults are preset and free. Conditional Access gives granular if-then policies and needs a premium license.")
D(4,"Something you have","Something you are","A fingerprint or face scan.",1,"Biometrics are something you are. Phones and keys are something you have.")
# ---- 5 Access management
C(5,"What is Conditional Access?","Entra's policy engine: **if-then** rules that take **signals**, make a **decision**, and **enforce** it. Example: if a user signs in from outside the country, then require MFA.")
C(5,"Conditional Access: name common signals.","**User or group**, **location** (IP range, country), **device** (platform, compliance), **application**, and **real-time sign-in or user risk** from ID Protection.")
C(5,"Conditional Access: what decisions can it make?","**Block access**, or **grant access**, optionally with requirements: MFA, a compliant device, a hybrid joined device, an approved app, or a password change.")
C(5,"Microsoft Entra roles vs Azure roles (Azure RBAC)?","**Entra roles** control access to **Entra resources**: users, groups, apps, licenses (for example Global Administrator, User Administrator). **Azure roles** control access to **Azure resources** such as VMs and storage (Owner, Contributor, Reader).")
C(5,"Built-in vs custom Entra roles?","**Built-in:** fixed permission sets ready to use. **Custom:** you pick the permissions yourself, which needs a premium license. Always give the **least privileged** role that does the job.")
C(5,"What is Global Secure Access?","Microsoft's **Security Service Edge (SSE)** solution: **Microsoft Entra Internet Access** (secure access to internet and SaaS) and **Microsoft Entra Private Access** (secure access to private apps without a VPN).")
C(5,"At which scopes can an Azure role be assigned?","**Management group, subscription, resource group, or a single resource.** Access is inherited downward.")
Q(5,"You must require MFA only when users sign in from outside the corporate network. Which feature should you use?","Conditional Access",["Security defaults","Self-service password reset","Access reviews"],"Conditional Access applies controls based on signals such as location. Security defaults cannot target conditions.")
Q(5,"In a Conditional Access policy, what is the user's sign-in location an example of?","A signal",["A decision","An enforcement","A role assignment"],"Signals are the inputs (user, location, device, app, risk). The decision is block or grant. Enforcement applies it.")
Q(5,"A help desk worker must reset user passwords in Microsoft Entra ID but must not manage Azure virtual machines. Which kind of role should you assign?","A Microsoft Entra role",["An Azure role at subscription scope","A managed identity","A sensitivity label"],"Entra roles manage directory objects such as users and passwords. Azure roles manage Azure resources.")
Q(5,"Which role has the broadest permissions across Microsoft Entra ID and should be limited to very few people?","Global Administrator",["User Administrator","Billing Administrator","Reader"],"Global Administrator can manage everything in Entra ID. Least privilege means few people hold it.")
Q(5,"Which outcome can a Conditional Access policy NOT produce by itself?","Automatically deleting the user account",["Blocking access","Granting access with MFA required","Granting access only from a compliant device"],"Conditional Access blocks or grants access with requirements. It does not delete accounts.")
Q(5,"Which TWO can be used as signals in a Conditional Access policy?",["The device platform","The application being accessed"],["The size of the user's mailbox","The Azure region of a VM"],"Common signals are user or group, location, device, application and risk.")
M(5,"Match each Conditional Access example to its part of the policy.",[("The user signs in from an unfamiliar country","Signal"),("Grant access, but require MFA","Decision"),("The requirement is applied when the user opens the app","Enforcement")],"Signals are inputs, the decision is block or grant with requirements, and enforcement applies it at access time.")
D(5,"Microsoft Entra roles","Azure roles","Owner, Contributor and Reader on a resource group.",1,"Those are Azure RBAC roles for Azure resources. Entra roles manage directory objects.")
D(5,"Signal","Decision","Block access, or grant access with MFA.",1,"Block and grant are decisions. Signals are the inputs such as user, location and risk.")
D(5,"Microsoft Entra Internet Access","Microsoft Entra Private Access","Reach internal company apps without a traditional VPN.",1,"Private Access is for private apps. Internet Access secures internet and SaaS traffic.")
# ---- 6 Identity protection and governance
C(6,"What is Microsoft Entra ID Governance for?","Making sure the **right people have the right access to the right resources at the right time**. Covers the identity lifecycle (join, move, leave), access lifecycle and privileged access lifecycle.")
C(6,"What is entitlement management?","Bundles the groups, apps and sites a role needs into an **access package**. Users request it, approval and **expiration** are built in. Works for guests too.")
C(6,"What are access reviews?","Scheduled checks where owners, managers or users themselves **confirm who still needs access** to groups, apps or roles. Access not confirmed can be removed automatically.")
C(6,"What does Privileged Identity Management (PIM) do?","Makes admin roles **just-in-time and time-bound**. Users are *eligible* and must **activate** the role, which can require **approval, MFA and a justification**. Everything is audited.")
C(6,"What is Microsoft Entra ID Protection?","Detects identity-based risks with Microsoft's threat signals, lets you **investigate** them in reports, and **remediates** automatically through risk-based Conditional Access.")
C(6,"ID Protection: user risk vs sign-in risk?","**Sign-in risk:** chance that this one sign-in is not the real owner (anonymous IP, atypical travel, password spray). **User risk:** chance that the identity itself is compromised (for example leaked credentials).")
C(6,"How do you auto-remediate risk in ID Protection?","Risk-based **Conditional Access**: medium or high **sign-in risk** requires MFA; high **user risk** requires a secure **password change**, or blocks access.")
Q(6,"Administrators should hold the Global Administrator role only for the hour they need it, after approval. Which feature provides this?","Privileged Identity Management",["Access reviews","Microsoft Entra ID Protection","Security defaults"],"PIM gives just-in-time, time-bound, approval-based role activation.")
Q(6,"Every quarter, group owners must confirm that members still need access, and unconfirmed members should be removed. What should you use?","Access reviews",["Privileged Identity Management","Conditional Access","Self-service password reset"],"Access reviews recertify access on a schedule and can remove access automatically.")
Q(6,"A user's credentials are found in a leaked list on the dark web. Which type of risk does Microsoft Entra ID Protection raise?","User risk",["Sign-in risk","Compliance risk","Insider risk"],"Leaked credentials suggest the identity itself is compromised, which is user risk. Sign-in risk is about a specific sign-in attempt.")
Q(6,"A sign-in comes from an anonymous IP address in a country the user has never visited. Which risk type is this?","Sign-in risk",["User risk","Secure score","Compliance score"],"Sign-in risk is the probability that a given sign-in was not performed by the identity owner.")
Q(6,"New project members need a set of groups, an app and a SharePoint site, with access that expires after 90 days. Which feature bundles this?","Entitlement management access packages",["Microsoft Entra Connect Sync","Custom banned password list","Azure Bastion"],"Access packages bundle resources with approval and expiration policies.")
Q(6,"Which TWO can PIM require before a user activates an eligible role?",["Multifactor authentication","Approval from a designated approver"],["A new device purchase","Deleting their mailbox"],"PIM activation can require MFA, justification and approval, and is time-limited.")
YN(6,["Privileged Identity Management provides time-bound access to roles.","Access reviews can only be completed by Global Administrators.","Microsoft Entra ID Protection can feed risk into Conditional Access policies."],[1,0,1],"PIM is just-in-time and time-bound. Reviewers can be owners, managers or the users themselves. Risk is a Conditional Access signal.")
D(6,"Privileged Identity Management","Access reviews","Activate an admin role for two hours with approval.",0,"PIM handles just-in-time activation. Access reviews recertify existing access.")
D(6,"User risk","Sign-in risk","Atypical travel detected on one sign-in.",1,"Atypical travel relates to a specific sign-in. User risk is about the identity being compromised.")
D(6,"Microsoft Entra ID Protection","Microsoft Entra ID Governance","Detects leaked credentials and risky sign-ins.",0,"ID Protection detects and remediates risk. ID Governance manages who has access and for how long.")
